course: dictionary
lesson: 4775
title: One word, many places
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning. The words around it are the clues. «The kitchen is the hub of our home.» Here a hub is not a wheel part. It is the busiest, most important centre, where everyone gathers.
explain: Now read this: «Ball bearings, tiny steel balls inside a wheel's hub, let the wheel turn on its axle with very little friction.» The clues are wheel and axle, so hub must mean the middle part of a wheel, where the spokes join.
explain: Ask yourself which meaning fits. Then swap your meaning into the sentence. If «the busiest centre of our home» still makes sense, you have the right one. In geography, «a transport hub» is a busy place like an airport or station.
fact: Hub is a noun. It names a thing: one hub, two hubs.
fact: A hub is the centre of a wheel, where the spokes or axle are joined.
fact: A hub is also the busiest or most important centre of an activity or place, such as «a transport hub».
fact: In computing, a hub is a device that joins several cables or computers together, like a USB hub.
fact: The same word can have different meanings. The context tells you which one to choose.
exercise: «The kitchen is the hub of our home.» What does hub mean here? | A small wooden box | The middle of a bicycle wheel | A type of cable | *The busiest, most important centre
exercise: «Ball bearings sit inside a wheel's hub.» Which part of a bike is the hub? | The brake lever | The bell | *The centre of the wheel | The seat
exercise: Which of these is a transport hub? | A quiet garden shed | A bag of sweets | A snowy hill | *A busy airport
test: In a USB hub, what does the hub do? | It cuts paper | It makes the computer lighter | It plays music | *It joins several devices together
test: Sam says a bike hub and an airport can both be called hubs. Why does that make sense? | Both are old words nobody uses | Both are made of steel and spin round | Both are only found in cities | *Both are central places where things meet and join
exam: «The hub of the wheel turned, and the bike rolled on.» What does hub mean here? | The busiest centre of town | A type of pet | A computer device that joins cables | *The centre of the wheel
